' ============================================================================
' Manilov__ForwardJumpSequence.puml
'
' Purpose : The sequence of nftables table/chain jumps on the @Manilov VM path
'           (@terra, bridge profile): where the image's drop-only tables sit
'           relative to the host's scoped forward permit, and why the
'           MANILOV_FORWARD jump must precede the first Docker jump.
' Source  : docs/adr/0006-ManilovNetworkOwnership.md — D4 (firewall split),
'           D4a (host permit MANILOV_FORWARD), §6b OQ5; ADR 0007 D1/D3.
' Scope   : structure and ordering only. No concrete interface names, no
'           addresses, no live rule handles. The live Docker jump order is
'           deliberately NOT drawn: it is read from
'             nft -a list chain inet filter FORWARD
'           and it is the rule-handle order, not anything in this file.
' Invariants:
'   * Two independent traversals. (1) BASE-CHAIN walk at a hook: every base
'     chain registered on that hook is traversed in priority order. (2) RULE
'     walk inside one base chain: an ordered list, evaluated by handle.
'   * At one hook an `accept` is not final; a `drop` is final; a jump cannot
'     cross tables — so the permit cannot be jumped from the image table.
'   * br_netfilter (bridge-nf-call-*) makes a bridged frame traverse the IP
'     forward hook too. br_netfilter's IP-forward shim is registered at
'     NF_BR_FORWARD with priority NF_BR_PRI_FIRST (INT_MIN), which is ahead of
'     every configurable bridge-family priority — including the image table's
'     -10. Hence the IP forward hook runs BEFORE the bridge-family forward
'     chains. Within one hook, priority order decides (image -10 before host 0).
'   * At the IP forward hook a bridged frame's iif/oif are the bridge PORTS,
'     not `br0`. Port placeholders are used here on purpose.
'
' Render  : plantuml -tpng -tsvg docs/_diagrams/Manilov__ForwardJumpSequence.puml
' ============================================================================

@startuml
!include _shared/DeployCommon.puml

title @Manilov VM path — nftables table/chain jump sequence\n(host permit vs image drops; live Docker order = OQ5, unknown)

skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDF0
skinparam noteBorderColor #888888
skinparam wrapWidth 640
skinparam maxMessageSize 420
skinparam noteTextAlignment left

participant "packet\n(one frame)" as PKT
participant "hook: bridge/\nforward\n(base chains,\nby priority)" as BRF
participant "hook: inet/\nforward\n(base chains,\nby priority)" as IPF
participant "inet filter FORWARD\none base chain\n(rules, by handle)" as FWD
participant "regular chains\nMANILOV_FORWARD\nDOCKER-*" as REG
participant "egress\nport" as OUT

== S0 — Legend: what a verdict means ==
note over PKT, OUT
  TRAVERSAL 1 — base chains at a hook, ordered by PRIORITY (every base chain on the hook is traversed).
  TRAVERSAL 2 — rules inside ONE base chain, ordered by HANDLE.
  `accept` in a base chain is NOT final (the next base chain still runs); a `drop` is final; a base-chain policy
  applies only when its own rule list ends with no verdict; a jump cannot cross tables.
end note

== S1 — VM -> LAN: traversal 1, base chains at each hook ==
PKT -> BRF : S1 frame in on the tap PORT of br0
note over PKT, OUT
  S0.5 (nothing decided here): bridge/PREROUTING -> br_nf_pre_routing (INT_MIN)
  -> IP prerouting, which is conntrack only.
  br_nf_forward_ip sits at NF_BR_FORWARD with priority NF_BR_PRI_FIRST (INT_MIN), so the IP forward hook (S2/S3)
  runs BEFORE the bridge-family FORWARD chains — the image one is at -10.
end note

BRF -> IPF : S2 br_netfilter (br_nf_forward_ip at NF_BR_PRI_FIRST)\ncalls the IP forward hook from inside the bridge forward path
note over PKT, OUT
  S3 base chains at inet/forward, in PRIORITY order:
     prio -10  table inet manilov_vm      (image: DROPS ONLY, policy accept) -> no drop matches -> accept (NOT final)
     prio   0  table inet filter FORWARD  (host) -> S4, traversal 2
  Base chains of other families at this hook (e.g. an `ip`-family Docker FORWARD) are ordered by priority, then by
  registration order: read them live. A `drop` anywhere here kills the frame immediately.
end note

IPF -> FWD : S4 enter the host FORWARD rule list

== S2 — VM -> LAN: traversal 2, rules inside inet filter FORWARD ==
note over PKT, OUT
  S4 rules are evaluated in HANDLE order; contract-relevant shape only:
     #1  jump MANILOV_FORWARD          <- must precede the first Docker jump
     #2  jump DOCKER-USER              <- actual identity/handle: OQ5, see S6
     #3  jump DOCKER-FORWARD
     #4  jump DOCKER-ISOLATION-STAGE-1
         (end of rule list -> policy drop)
  A `jump` to a regular chain RETURNS on completion, but a `drop` or an `accept` reached inside FORWARD itself
  TERMINATES this rule list. A `goto` (not drawn) never returns at all.
end note

FWD -> REG : S5 jump MANILOV_FORWARD (position #1)
note over PKT, OUT
  S5 MANILOV_FORWARD is a REGULAR chain in table inet filter, scoped to the declared pair only:
     iifname "<tap-port>"    oifname "<uplink-port>"  accept
     iifname "<uplink-port>" oifname "<tap-port>"     accept
  The placeholders are bridge PORTS: at this hook a bridged frame is not seen with `br0` as iif/oif.
  VERIFY ON THE LIVE HOST before trusting the pair: `nft -a list chain inet filter FORWARD` plus
  `bridge link` / `ip -d link show <tap>`. Recorded as OQ5b in ADR 0006 §6b; the contract is unchanged.
end note

REG --> FWD : S6 accept returned to FORWARD; FORWARD ends here,\nso its policy drop is never reached for this packet
IPF -> BRF : S7 back to bridge/forward: bridge-family base chains, by priority
note over PKT, OUT
  S7 prio -10 table bridge manilov_vm (image: DROPS ONLY, policy accept) -> no drop matches -> accept.
  A `drop` HERE is final even though the IP hook already accepted, so the image's anti-spoof/isolation drops still
  apply after the host permit. The reverse is impossible: this table can never permit anything.
end note

BRF -> OUT : S8 bridge/POSTROUTING -> IP postrouting -> frame out on the uplink PORT

== S3 — LAN -> VM: mirror walk, same two traversals ==
PKT -> BRF : S1' frame in on the uplink PORT of br0
BRF -> IPF : S2' br_netfilter -> IP forward hook
note over PKT, OUT
  S3' prio -10 table inet manilov_vm (image drops) -> no match -> accept;
      prio   0 table inet filter FORWARD -> jump MANILOV_FORWARD (#1) -> accept (iif uplink, oif tap)
               -> FORWARD ends; the Docker jumps are never evaluated.
  S7' bridge/forward: table bridge manilov_vm (prio -10), drops only -> S8' out on the tap PORT -> @Manilov virtio.
end note

== S4 — FAILURE: permit jump placed after a Docker jump ==
PKT -> FWD : S4 frame enters FORWARD; rules evaluated in handle order
FWD -> REG : jump DOCKER-USER (#1) ...\njump DOCKER-FORWARD (#2) ...\njump MANILOV_FORWARD (#3) <- never reached
alt a Docker chain returns drop
  note over PKT, OUT
    DROP is final: the frame dies inside the IP hook. The bridge-family image drops never even run. VM unreachable.
  end note
else no rule matches; the rule list ends
  note over PKT, OUT
    Base-chain policy drop -> final -> the frame dies inside br_nf_forward_ip; the bridge hook is never reached.
    VM unreachable, with no diagnostic beyond the counter on the policy.
  end note
else a Docker rule happens to accept
  note over PKT, OUT
    ACCEPT, but the permit was never evaluated: connectivity works by accident and the permit's interface-pair
    scoping is not applied. Docker's rule set, not the marker, decides.
  end note
end

== S5 — Who sits where: neither side can substitute for the other ==
note over PKT, OUT
  IMAGE (@NikolaiGogol applier): table bridge manilov_vm + table inet manilov_vm, hook forward,
    prio -10, policy accept, DROPS ONLY. Can kill a packet at any point; can never let one through;
    never reasserted by the host.
  HOST (bootstrap / host provisioning): regular chain MANILOV_FORWARD jumped from FORWARD before the
    first Docker jump, ACCEPT ONLY. The only object that carries the packet past the host `drop` policy,
    and the only one the host reasserts after reboot or a host-manager reassertion.
  Remove the permit -> VM unreachable (policy drop wins; the image drops are irrelevant).
  Remove the image drops -> forwarding works, anti-spoof/isolation is lost. Complementary, never alternatives.
end note

== S6 — OQ5: the one thing this diagram must NOT invent ==
note over PKT, OUT
  The decision fixes ONE position: our jump precedes the FIRST Docker jump.
  It does not fix the relative order of DOCKER-USER, DOCKER-FORWARD,
  DOCKER-ISOLATION-STAGE-1, or any TOS-managed rule. That is OQ5, and it is resolved only by reading the live chain:
         nft -a list chain inet filter FORWARD
  Both shapes below are contract-conforming, and both are UNKNOWN until read:
     variant A: ours, DOCKER-USER, DOCKER-FORWARD, DOCKER-ISOLATION-STAGE-1
     variant B: TOS-rule, ours, DOCKER-FORWARD, DOCKER-USER, ...
  The invariant is only `ours` before the first `jump|goto DOCKER-*` handle.
end note

legend right
  Two traversals, never conflated:
    1. base chains at a hook -> by priority
    2. rules inside a chain  -> by handle
  accept at a hook = not final.  drop = final.
  a jump cannot cross tables.
  OQ5 = live Docker jump order, read from `nft -a list chain inet filter FORWARD`.
end legend

@enduml
